  "hints": [
    "Without the loop this is the classic \"no two adjacent\" maximum sum along a line. What new conflict does the loop add?",
    "The only new conflict is between the first and last stop, so every valid choice skips at least one of them. Solve both cases as straight lines.",
    "Answer = max(line answer for stops 1..n-1, line answer for stops 0..n-2). A line answer keeps two running values: best total if the current stop is taken, and best if it is skipped. Handle a single stop separately."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function maxCircularLoot(houses) {\n  const line = (from, to) => {\n    let take = 0\n    let skip = 0\n    for (let i = from; i < to; i++) {\n      const nextTake = skip + houses[i]\n      skip = Math.max(take, skip)\n      take = nextTake\n    }\n    return Math.max(take, skip)\n  }\n  if (houses.length === 1) return houses[0]\n  return Math.max(line(1, houses.length), line(0, houses.length - 1))\n}\n",
    "py": "def max_circular_loot(houses):\n    def line(start, stop):\n        take, skip = 0, 0\n        for i in range(start, stop):\n            take, skip = skip + houses[i], max(take, skip)\n        return max(take, skip)\n\n    if len(houses) == 1:\n        return houses[0]\n    return max(line(1, len(houses)), line(0, len(houses) - 1))\n",
    "explanation": "Any valid choice skips the first stop or skips the last stop, so the circle splits into two straight-line problems. Each line uses the non-adjacent-sum recurrence with two rolling values: take = previous skip + value, skip = max(previous take, previous skip). The larger of the two line answers is the result."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(1)",
    "notes": "Two linear passes over index ranges; no copies of the array are needed."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "How would you report which stops were chosen?",
    "What if the stops form a tree of roads and directly connected stops cannot both be emptied?"
  ]
}
